Fertilizer. The Petroleum segment refines and markets transportation fuels, such as gasoline, diesel, jet fuel, and distillates; and includes crude gathering and logistics activities that support refinery operations. This segment also owns and operates a coking, medium-sour crude oil refinery in Kansas; and a crude oil refinery in Oklahoma. This segment serves retailers, railroads, farm cooperatives, and other refiners/marketers. The Renewables segment refines renewable feedstocks, including soybean oil, corn oil, and other renewable feedstocks into renewable diesel; and marketing of renewables products. The Nitrogen Fertilizer segment owns and operates a nitrogen fertilizer plant in Coffeyville, Kansas that utilizes a pet coke gasification process to produce nitrogen fertilizer products; and a nitrogen fertilizer facility in East Dubuque, Illinois that produces nitrogen fertilizers in the form of ammonia and urea ammonium nitrate (UAN) and nitric acid. This segment primarily markets UAN products to agricultural customers; and ammonia products to agricultural and industrial customers. The company was founded in 1906 and is headquartered in Sugar Land, Texas. CVR Energy, Inc. is a subsidiary of Icahn Enterprises Holdings L.P.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Michael H. Wright Jr. | Executive VP & COO | Age 55 | Compensation: $1.3M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 1K people. Revenue per employee of $4.7M ranks in the above the sector median among Energy peers. Stock-based compensation of $27K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 0\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (9/10).","financial_summary":["CVR Energy Inc. is a mid-cap energy business with a $3.8B market cap on $7.2B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at -13% over the past three years and +3% annually across the full decade, and a shrinking top line is the hardest headwind any business can overcome.","Returns on capital run at 10% against a 9% cost of capital, which means the business barely earns its keep economically.","The company is burning through $41M of cash, and without positive owner earnings any valuation rests on hope rather than evidence.","On the balance sheet, debt is moderate at 2.1\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, serviceable in normal conditions but worth watching through a downturn, and thin 1.3\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield is only 1.0%, a thin return of capital, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome, and profit capture is only 0.2\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Very elevated short interest (20.4% of float) \u2014 sophisticated money is betting against the business","sev":"red","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Operating margins 13% below sector median \u2014 a cost disadvantage the moat cannot hide","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Altman Z in the grey zone \u2014 financial health warrants monitoring","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Revenue growth lags most Energy peers \u2014 losing share inside the profit pool","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Cyclical sectors like Energy tend to underperform in slowdowns","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Above-average market volatility (VIX 26)","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Geopolitical supply shocks \u2014 wars, sanctions, and OPEC decisions create volatility that no hedging programme can fully neutralise.","sev":"amber","ca